      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the tavern's amber glow. Morning air hits your face \u2014 damp and cool, carrying the scent of wet stone and something older, something metallic.\n\nThe street curves north through a district of shuttered workshops and empty stalls. Cobbles glisten with last night's rain. A few early risers pass with heads down, hoods drawn, none meeting your gaze.\n\nAt the district's edge the buildings fall away, and you see it.\n\nThe Ash Gate stands alone against a pale grey sky \u2014 a cracked black arch, maybe fifteen feet tall, its keystone worn smooth by decades of weather. No door hangs in its frame. Beyond it, a thick curtain of grey mist churns slowly, swallowing whatever lies past the threshold. The ground before it is churned mud crisscrossed with old boot prints, some half-erased by rain.\n\nA small wooden landing juts from the mud at the arch's base \u2014 the ferry mooring \u2014 but no boat sits there. Only a single frayed rope, still tied to a rusted iron ring, trailing into the motionless mist.\n\nThe silence here is heavier than at the tavern. No birds. No wind. Just the faint, almost imperceptible sound of the mist breathing."
    },
