      "narrative": "You drop to a crouch at the threshold, fingers brushing the cold stone beneath the arch. The mist hangs overhead like a held breath, close enough to touch, but you keep your attention low.\n\nThere. On the dark stone of the arch's base \u2014 faint, parallel scratches, barely visible against the weathered surface. Fresh enough that rain hasn't blurred them entirely. Something metal was dragged across this stone recently. Heavy, too \u2014 the marks are deeper on one end, shallower on the other, as if whatever made them was hauled *out* from beneath the arch.\n\nYour eyes trace down to the dirt. A shallow indentation presses into the mud beside the scratches \u2014 a rectangular depression, its edges softened by a few days of weather. A crate? A chest? Something with a flat base, dragged across the stone and then set down here before being moved again.\n\nYou straighten and turn to the frayed rope trailing into the mist. Up close, the damage is wrong. The fibers aren't worn thin by weather and salt \u2014 they're cleanly severed in places, snapped at odd angles. Some strands are stretched long and thin before breaking, others cut short. This rope didn't rot. It was *cut*, or snapped under a sudden, violent pull from below.\n\nThe landing is empty. The ferry is gone. And the evidence at your feet tells a story that doesn't match the official one."
