      "narrative": "The walk back is measured, your boots finding the same cobbles you crossed earlier. The morning has warmed a fraction, but the sun remains hidden behind a flat white sky. A few more figures move through the streets now\u2014a woman carrying bread, a man hauling a handcart stacked with empty crates\u2014but none spare you a glance.\n\nThe Copper Finch's door groans as you push it open. The tavern is quieter than before. A single patron sits near the window, nursing a small cup, eyes fixed on the street outside. Behind the bar, Mara Venn looks up from wiping a glass, her sharp gaze tracking you as you cross the scarred floorboards.\n\nYour old table stands against the wall\u2014the one with the deep groove in its surface, the one near the hearth where the fire has burned down to orange embers. The chair scrapes as you pull it out. The wood is worn smooth by years of elbows and tankards.\n\nMara sets the glass down and walks around the bar, drying her hands on her apron. She doesn't speak at first, just watches you settle.\n\n\"Didn't expect you back so soon,\" she says. Her tone is neutral, but there's a question tucked into it. \"Gate still sealed, I take it?\""
